rements on platforms that host user-generated content."},{"industry":"media-entertainment","confidence":0.4,"rationale":"Online publishers and digital media platforms may face compliance obligations if they host user-generated intimate content."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would make it a federal crime to publish intimate images or videos of someone online without their consent, including computer-generated deepfakes, when the publication is intended to cause harm or involves a minor. It would require websites and apps that host user-generated content to remove such images within 48 hours of being notified by the person depicted. Violators would face criminal penalties including prison time, fines, and mandatory restitution to victims.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Children, and the adults responsible for them. Anyone who uses the internet.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"O000019","score":0.06258174186191032},"overlapCount":39},"meaning":{"percentile":41.867802492506705,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.6282886178448903}},{"billId":"119-hr-4235","bioguideId":"G000589","cycle":2026,"score":0.008314406176907124,"matches":[{"industry":"legal","donorAmount":17250,"donorShare":0.01347754172764115,"billConfidence":0.85,"contribution":0.007637273645663318},{"industry":"media-entertainment","donorAmount":2000,"donorShare":0.0015626135336395536,"billConfidence":0.65,"contribution":0.0006771325312438065}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.6282886178448903},"bill":{"id":"119-hr-4235","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"4235","title":"To clarify the Holocaust Expropriated Art Recovery Act of 2016, to appropriately limit the application of defenses based on the passage of time and other non-merits defenses to claims under that Act.","introducedDate":"2025-06-27","latestActionDate":"2025-06-27","latestActionText":"Referred to the House Committee on the Judiciary.","policyArea":"Law","subjects":["Art, artists, authorship","Civil actions and liability","Conflicts and wars","Crime victims","Historical and cultural resources","Museums, exhibitions, cultural centers","War crimes, genocide, crimes against humanity","World history"],"sponsorBioguideId":"L000597","cosponsorCount":29,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/4235","industries":[{"industry":"legal","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"The bill expands litigation procedures, jurisdictional reach, and limits defenses in civil claims, directly affecting law firms handling art recovery and international litigation."},{"industry":"media-entertainment","confidence":0.65,"rationale":"Museums and cultural institutions holding artwork are subject to expanded liability and jurisdictional reach under the modified claims procedures."}],"plainSummary":"This bill removes the 2026 deadline for filing lawsuits to recover art and property stolen during the Holocaust, allowing claims to be filed indefinitely as long as they are brought within six years of discovering the item. It makes it easier to sue foreign governments in U.S. courts and to serve legal papers on defendants anywhere in the country. The bill also blocks certain legal defenses, such as arguments that too much time has passed or that U.S. courts should defer to foreign laws, for both current and future cases.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"People who have been the victim of a crime.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"N000002","score":0.032950606363763804},"overlapCount":28},"meaning":{"percentile":40.13251301467108,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[{"kind":"sta